---
import Avatar from "@/components/Avatar.astro";
import ShareLink from "@/components/ShareLink.astro";
import SocialList from "@/components/SocialList.astro";
import { getLocaleFromUrl, ui } from "@/i18n";
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

const locale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url);
const t = ui[locale];
const meta = {
	description: t.aboutDescription,
	title: t.about,
};
---

<PageLayout meta={meta}>
	<div class="grid items-start gap-8 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_13rem] md:gap-12">
		<div>
			<h1 class="title mb-12">{t.about}</h1>
			<div class="prose prose-reading prose-cactus max-w-none">
				{
					locale === "en" ? (
						<>
							<p>In the long river of time, to my left is Einstein’s dream of the universe and the unknown; to my right, Nalan Xingde’s reflections on words and the human world. At the center of that current is the person I am, carried downstream yet sometimes turning back to seek the source, somewhere between reason and poetry.</p>
							<p>I was once captivated by literature, and I remain drawn to the different ways of being alive behind words—not merely springtime sorrow and parting grief, but echoes of the human world in all its variety.</p>
							<p>I admire Tao Yuanming’s quiet detachment in “Picking chrysanthemums by the eastern fence, I catch sight of the southern mountain”; Li Bai’s exuberance in “Laughing to the sky, I walk out the door—how could one such as I be a common man?”; and Su Shi’s composure in “A bamboo staff and straw sandals are lighter than a horse—who is afraid? In a straw cloak, I’ll walk through wind and rain.” Each meets the world with a different kind of freedom.</p>
							<p>I am equally drawn to the compassion in Du Fu’s grave, measured verse, the fierce sincerity behind Xin Qiji’s visions of warhorses and battlefields, and the tenderness in Nalan Xingde’s lucid lines. Some withdrew to their fields; some set out with swords at their side. Some carried the fate of the realm in their hearts; others watched the human world through rain at dusk. Literature preserves hundreds of ways to live, allowing me to cross time and meet distant yet vivid souls.</p>
							<p>Literature is more than a refuge for emotion. It reveals the subtleties of individual lives and the weight of an age. It teaches me to hear the silence beyond words, to understand the circumstances behind a point of view, and to keep both tenderness and clarity in a complicated world.</p>
							<p>I, too, once gazed up in wonder at the depths of the physical world, beginning with a deceptively simple question: Why does the world work the way it does?</p>
							<p>How does light cross the vastness of the universe? How is a star born, and how does it fall silent? Why do speed and gravity alter the passage of time? Why does the quantum world reveal itself only through probabilities? Such questions remind me that beneath familiar reality lies an order far larger than intuition.</p>
							<p>I am fascinated by Newton’s use of mathematics to describe the laws shared by planets and dust alike, and by Einstein’s weaving of time, space, and gravity into a single picture. I marvel at the quantum challenge to certainty, and at the symmetry, conservation, and unity disclosed by compact equations.</p>
							<p>Physics does not diminish the poetry of the world; it reveals a deeper kind of romance. The starlight reaching our eyes may have traveled for millions of years. The elements in our bodies were forged inside ancient stars. And with finite lives and limited reason, we can still try to understand a universe immeasurably larger than ourselves.</p>
							<p>Physics teaches me to question, and also to be humble. Every answer opens another question; every arrival reveals a wider unknown. The universe does not fall silent because we are puzzled. Exploration is the answer we offer in return.</p>
							<p>Ultimately, following the glimmer of thought Turing left behind, I entered the world of computing and traveled ever farther along that road. Literature gives me the sensitivity to feel the world, physics trains me in how to question it, and computing gives me tools with which to build. They may seem to belong to feeling, reason, and practice, yet in my life they ultimately join the same river.</p>
							<p>I also enjoy looking back through the long scroll of history. The rise and fall of dynasties, the passage of civilizations, and the fortunes of individuals are more than stories sealed in the past; they are recurring echoes of humanity and its times. Reading history helps me understand the present on a larger scale—and reminds me that many of today’s uncertainties have been lived through before, in other forms.</p>
							<p>Science and art are two sides of the same whole: science asks why the world is as it is, while art answers why life can move us. Reason gives imagination its boundaries, and imagination opens new possibilities for reason. History tells us where we came from; reflection on life helps us choose where to go.</p>
							<p>Life may not have a single answer. More important than arriving is to remain curious within our finite years: to feel deeply, keep asking, and create with our own hands something worth leaving behind.</p>
							<p>The journey has its course, but exploration knows no bounds. Here I record the words I have read, the code I have written, and the thoughts gathered along the way.</p>
							<h2>About this blog</h2>
							<p>Hi, I’m Nalansitan（纳兰斯坦）.</p>
							<p>This is my personal blog for software engineering, reading notes, and long-term thinking.</p>
							<p>Good writing is more than publishing conclusions; it is also a way to organize how I think. I hope these notes remain useful to my future self and to anyone who happens to visit.</p>
							<p>The site uses Astro Cactus, Markdown, and pnpm, and is published to GitHub Pages with GitHub Actions.</p>
						</>
					) : (
						<>
							<p>在时光的长河里，左手边，是爱因斯坦关于宇宙与未知的梦想；右手边，是纳兰容若关于文字与人间的心绪。河流中央，是独一无二的我，在理性与诗意之间，顺流而下，也溯流求索。</p>
							<p>我曾痴迷文学，至今仍眷恋文字背后各不相同的生命姿态——那不只是伤春悲秋、离愁别绪，更是人间万千气象的回响。</p>
							<p>喜欢陶渊明“采菊东篱下，悠然见南山”的淡泊，在尘世之外守住内心的从容；喜欢李白“仰天大笑出门去，我辈岂是蓬蒿人”的豪迈，以天真和狂放回应世间的拘束；喜欢苏轼“竹杖芒鞋轻胜马，谁怕？一蓑烟雨任平生”的旷达，在风雨之中依然保持清醒与自在。</p>
							<p>也喜欢杜甫沉郁顿挫中的悲悯，辛弃疾金戈铁马背后的赤诚，纳兰容若清丽词句里的深情。有人归隐田园，有人仗剑远游；有人心怀天下，有人在细雨黄昏中凝望人间。文字容纳了生命的千百种形态，也让我得以越过时间，与那些遥远而鲜活的灵魂相遇。</p>
							<p>文学并非只是情绪的寄托。它让人看见个体的幽微，也让人理解时代的重量；它教我感受语言之外的沉默，体察立场背后的处境，并在纷繁世界中保留一份柔软与清醒。</p>
							<p>我也曾仰望物理世界的深邃，从一个看似简单的问题出发：世界为何如此运行？</p>
							<p>一束光如何穿越漫长的宇宙，一颗星辰如何诞生又归于寂静，时间为何会因速度与引力而改变，微观粒子为何只能以概率向我们显现——这些问题让我意识到，习以为常的现实背后，隐藏着远比直觉更辽阔的秩序。</p>
							<p>我着迷于牛顿用数学描述天体与尘埃共同遵循的运动规律，也着迷于爱因斯坦将时间、空间与引力重新编织为同一幅图景；我惊叹于量子世界对确定性的挑战，也敬畏那些简洁方程背后所揭示的对称、守恒与统一。</p>
							<p>物理并没有削弱世界的诗意，反而让我看见另一种更深的浪漫：星光抵达眼前时，或许已经在宇宙中跋涉了千万年；构成身体的元素，曾在古老恒星的内部诞生；而我们竟能以有限的生命和理性，尝试理解这个远比自身宏大的世界。</p>
							<p>物理教我追问，也教我谦逊。每一个答案都会打开新的问题，每一次抵达都意味着更辽阔的未知。宇宙并不因人的困惑而沉默，而探索本身，就是我们给予它的回应。</p>
							<p>最终，我却循着图灵留下的思想微光，走进计算机的世界，并在这条道路上越走越远。文学赋予我感受世界的触角，物理训练我追问世界的方式，计算机则让我拥有亲手构建世界的工具。它们看似分属感性、理性与实践，最终却在我的生命里汇入同一条河流。</p>
							<p>我也喜欢在历史的长卷中回望来路。王朝兴替、文明流转、人物沉浮，不只是尘封的往事，也是人性与时代一次次留下的回声。读历史，让我在宏大的时间尺度中理解当下，也让我看见：许多今日的困惑，前人早已以不同的方式经历过。</p>
							<p>我始终相信，科学与艺术是一体两面：科学追问世界何以如此，艺术回应生命何以动人；理性为想象确立边界，想象又为理性打开新的可能。历史让我们知道从何处来，而对人生的思考，则引导我们决定向何处去。</p>
							<p>人生或许没有唯一的答案。比抵达更重要的，是在有限的岁月里保持好奇，认真感受，不断追问，并亲手创造一些值得留下的事物。</p>
							<p>山水有程，探索无涯。这里记录我读过的文字、写下的代码，以及一路拾得的思考。</p>
							<h2>关于这个博客</h2>
							<p>你好，我是纳兰斯坦、爱因容若。</p>
							<p>这是我的个人博客，用来记录技术实践、阅读笔记和长期思考。</p>
							<p>我相信好的写作不只是输出结论，也是在整理自己的认知。希望这里的内容对未来的我，以及偶然来到这里的你，都有一点价值。</p>
							<p>网站使用 Astro Cactus、Markdown 和 pnpm 构建，通过 GitHub Actions 发布到 GitHub Pages。</p>
						</>
					)
				}
			</div>
			<ShareLink />
			<div class="mt-8">
				<SocialList />
			</div>
		</div>
		<Avatar />
	</div>
</PageLayout>
